.SH A) OVERVIEW
unit-tests frequently use the same data or re-check the same results.
simple situations are handled with DITTO (see koios_cond) within a single
script, but across scripts and in complex situations requires shared code.
each of these shared sequences is ultimately translated as a single c-language
function, which in turn is called by scripts.

.SH B) USES
there are three types of shared code with their own identifiers depending on
the scope of sharing.

.SS SHARED
.nf
-- defined in any normal ".unit" file
-- can ONLY be accessed in that file
-- uses a [a-z] lower letter identifier
-- can not call itself or any other shared code

.SS GLOBAL
.nf
-- defined in either unit_wide.unit or unit_data.unit
-- can be accessed from any unit test script
-- uses a [A-Z] upper letter identifier
-- can not call itself or any other shared code

.SS CONFIG
.nf
-- defined in either unit_wide.unit or unit_data.unit
-- can be accessed from any unit test script
-- uses a [è-ÿ] greek letter identifier
-- can not call itself or any other shared code

i often use CONFIG for creating and evaluating large or complex data sets.
typically, i use GLOBAL for useful shared code without major data.

.SH C) EXAMPLE
all unit testing shared code must begin with the one of the three verbs show
in section B, and are a single, un-indented line containing five columns using
ascii field-seperator (0x1c) delimited. these field delimiters will be shown
here with the (§) character.

   verb-- § -desc---- § -label- § -legal- § ---titles----------------------- §
   CONFIG § set up    § yLOG    § åab···æ § a=dir-only·····, b=proj········· §

.SH D) VERB
SHARED, GLOBAL, or CONFIG are the verb and must have zero indentation.
this field is a maximum of twelve (12) characters, overflow and trailing
spaces will be truncated.

   verb-- § -desc---- § -label- § -legal- § ---titles----------------------- §
   CONFIG § ·         § ·       § ·       § ·                                §

.SH E) DESCRIPTION
it is very unhelpful to leave off a description as unit-testing almost
invariably detailed, complicated, and extensive.  descriptions are a maximum
of thirty-five (35) characters, overflow and trailing spaces will be truncated.
legal characters are letters, numbers, and most punctuation.

   verb-- § -desc---- § -label- § -legal- § ---titles----------------------- §
   CONFIG § set up    § ·       § ·       § ·                                §

.SH F) LABEL
my vim-based ide (vide) picks up these labels to provide an index on the
left-side of my editing window for rapid access.  labels are a maximum
of fourteen (14) characters, overflow and trailing spaces will be truncated.
legal characters are [a-zA-Z0-9_].

   verb-- § -desc---- § -label- § -legal- § ---titles----------------------- §
   CONFIG § ·         § yLOG    § ·       § ·                                §

.SH G) FILTERING
to maximize reuse, filtering allows a test to subtily or completely change
depending on what filter is requested.  filters are [a-z] lower case letters
that are later pre-pended on none, some, or all shared conditions and steps to
define sub-sets (please see koios_reuse for details).

.SH H) LEGAL
this filed indicates which specific filter identifiers are allowed -- '*'
means run everything and is always allowed.  legal is a maximumo of fourteen
(14) characters, overflow and trailing spaces will be truncated.  the format
is openning square brace, followed by [a-z·], and terminated by a closing
square brace.

   verb-- § -desc---- § -label- § -legal- § ---titles----------------------- §
   CONFIG § ·         § ·       § [ab···] § ·                                §

if only a '·' is provided, no legal filters are defined.

.SH I) TITLES
when running, filtered shared calls can be exceedingly ambiguous and
confusing unless each legal filter is given a short title for interal and
reporting purposes.  these are provided as a comma-seperated list.

   single title format "#=title········, "

where '#' is the filter identifier and title is a maximum thirteen (13)
character title.  trailing '·' are truncated.

   verb-- § -desc---- § -label- § -legal- § ---titles----------------------- §
   CONFIG § ·         § ·       § ·       § a=dir-only·····, b=proj········· §

.SH J) REUSE
this is actually covered in koios_reuse, but a little explanation is useful
here.  to call the shared code, use a REUSE verb.

   REUSE -a- §          runs an entire local SHARED section
   REUSE -B- §          runs an entire global GLOBAL section
   REUSE -ê- §          runs an entire global CONFIG section

   REUSE a/f §          runs just lines with 'f' filter in a SHARED section
   REUSE B/z §          runs just lines with 'z' filter in a GLOBAL section
   REUSE ê/b §          runs just lines with 'b' filter in a CONFIG section

.SH K) OUTPUT
normal reuse outputs for header and footer are shown below.  please refer to
KOIOS_WHICH (5) for filtered versions.  the example beflow is for a CONFIG
verb.

   CONFIG (÷/*) ==-------   set up details for sample polymnia   ---------==

